I'm experiencing similar when loading small effect scenes (typically an audio player or particle system).

```
var scenePath = effect.Scene();
if (scenePath is not null)
{
    if (ResourceLoader.Load<PackedScene>(scenePath) is {} packedScene
        && packedScene.Instantiate<Node3D>() is {} scene)
    { ...
```

There's a fairly limited number of effect options, so it's surprising. I have added in an Exists now, so I'll see if this reduces the issues - but not many players experience it.

@hhyyrylainen Can you still reproduce this issue? It looks like the PackedScene or its contents are getting corrupted. Since you say this happens usually after a long time playing the game, I wonder if it's the C# GC causing it.

We have found a similar issue that seems related to cached resources being disposed but still used because they are still in the cache:
- https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/83762

Could you try the workaround mentioned in this comment? (https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/83762#issuecomment-5319127215). The workaround is to use `CacheMode.Ignore` or `CacheMode.IgnoreDeep` so the resource is not cached and always recreated.

So, instead of loading a scene like this:

```csharp
var scene = GD.Load<PackedScene>($"res://src/thriveopedia/pages/Thriveopedia{name}Page.tscn");
page = scene.Instantiate<IThriveopediaPage>();
```

Try loading it like this:

```csharp
using var scene = (PackedScene)ResourceLoader.Load($"res://src/thriveopedia/pages/Thriveopedia{name}Page.tscn", ResourceLoader.CacheMode.IgnoreDeep);
page = scene.Instantiate<IThriveopediaPage>();
```

If you no longer see the issue after this, then it must be a duplicate of https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/83762.

After a bonkers amount of sniffing through crash reports and testing, I can confirm that the crashes happens when a GC.Collect() takes place while PackedScene.Instantiate() is running (thus the missing indexes from the log in my post above).

As noted, the crash only happens on release builds (and even more frequently on AOT builds), where optimizations tend to affect the lifetime of objects differently.

While I don't have a small repro project, I added a stress test to my game that forced it to quickly loaded packed scenes (that is then instantiated and added to the node tree) for 10 minutes straight. It eventually (often after a few minutes) crashed with the log messages mentioned above. The same thing happens on both 4.7.2 and on 4.8 beta 1.

To get it to crash deterministically, you can force a GC.Collect() from the constructor of the dynamically loaded packed scenes root script:

```csharp
public partial class SceneRoot : Node
{
    public SceneRoot()
    {
        GC.Collect();
        GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers();
    }
}
```

The "using" workaround suggested by @raulsntos works perfectly, but I landed on this instead since it should work even with other cache methods than IgnoreDeep (where we might not want to call Dispose() on it). Of course, using anyting other than IgnoreDeep assumes the dreaded "Handle is not initialized" bug gets fixed eventually. 

```csharp
var node = packedScene.Instantiate<Node>();
// The use of packedScene here stops the GC from removing it before Instantiate() has finished
GC.KeepAlive(packedScene);
```

While the real fix likely belongs in Godot's generated C# bindings, adding that one GC.KeepAlive() line causes both my deterministic and my 10 min stress test to pass 👍
